A1. A literary digest. ~ WASTEBASKET
A11. The world's gamest loser. ~ LIPTON
A12. The call of the wild (at a foot-ball game). ~ CHEERS
A14. What the faculties of some colleges should do to foot-ball games. ~ ATTEND
A15. This is pugnacious and stingy. ~ HORNET
A16. A popular visitor to Florida. ~ SUN
A17. This is all out of sorts today. ~ PI
A18. What she should have done to that ripping gown. ~ SEWN
A19. This is all run down this winter. ~ SAP
A21. This always lives in a trunk and is ready to leave in the Spring. ~ TREE
A23. This is able to keep even toothpaste from being a dry subject. ~ CAP
A25. A pleasant thing to do in a warm and cosy place when the outside world is cold and dreary. ~ NESTLE
A27. What the family never does since the new radio came. ~ RESTS
A29. This was seen and then it was sawn. (Does that sound sane?) ~ LOG
A31. What a bed time story does to sleep. ~ LULLS
A32. These are often held running around the Loop at all hours of the night. ~ ELS
A33. This pertains to your own Community. ~ PLATED
A36. What the house looks like the morning after. ~ MESS
A37. What he saw in her eyes. ~ YES
A38. After the crash this was the first to go into the hole. ~ RAT
A40. As singers these have been a howling success. ~ CATS
A44. The way a woman stands in a clothes-line argument. ~ AKIMBO
A46. An automobile mixture. ~ JAM
A48. An old fashioned girl. ~ HIRED
A50. A constellation. ~ ARA
A51. This always remains in Florida during the winter. ~ MIAMI
A53. Ready for the press. ~ EDITED
A55. You can have a picnic with this if you get in on the ground floor. ~ ANT
A57. The four letter burden of crossword puzzles. ~ ONUS
A58. The way he turned when he saw the motor cop. ~ RED
A59. A board of directors. ~ SIGN
A61. Because this is abbreviated it means half pay for someone. ~ HP
A63. Don't worry this is only a theory. ~ ISM
A64. To be down in the dumps. ~ REPINE
A66. What Floyd Gibbons can do 70 miles an hour. ~ RELATE
A68. This is ready to catch some one. ~ SNARER
A69. A crustacean having 7 pairs of legs of equal length. (This is a whole leg show in itself.—Page Mr. Ziegfeld.) ~ ISOPOD
A70. Although this has several high degrees it is down and out this winter. ~ THERMOMETER

D1. This proves it. ~ WITNESS
D2. Just as likely as not. ~ APT
D3. You will find this in every walk of life. ~ STEP
D4. An accelerator. ~ TONIC
D5. The last trick. Everyone would like to make this meet its double. ~ END
D6. Get a doctor to diagnose this. ~ ACHE
D7. Even ankles were not——in grandmother's day. ~ SHOWN
D8. This has an important date to fill next week. ~ KERNEL
D9. This is found even in poetry. ~ EEN
D10. A frame-up for bridge (accomplished by Work). ~ TRESTLE
D11. This is a winner. ~ LAUREL
D13. These are beastly apartments. ~ STALLS
D16. A take-off on the gridiron. ~ STRETCHER
D18. This is where the rub comes. ~ SPOT
D20. A disease that develops sore heads. ~ PESSIMISM
D22. This doesn't mean much in France. ~ ET
D24. The U.S. cold storage annex. ~ ALASKA
D26. This makes a good addition to this puzzle. ~ SUM
D28. A watch with hidden movements. ~ SPY
D30. The kind of a scientist Einstein is. ~ GERMAN
D34. A field of poetry that brings in the kine. ~ LEA
D35. What a school-girl will do for a complexion. ~ DAB
D39. A short thumb. ~ TOM
D41. What all the employed would like to be. ~ AIDERS
D42. The devil himself has got hold of this one. ~ TRIDENT
D43. This was made to bite on. ~ SET
D45. Persia. ~ IRAN
D46. This one is crazy with the heat. ~ JANITOR
D47. What Amos 'n' Andy have done for the Pepsodent Company. ~ AMUSED
D49. If this is a fur coat you probably will not get it. ~ DESIRE
D52. A peacock butterfly. (Doesn't that sound gorgeous?) ~ IO
D54. A car that gives good service morning, noon and night. ~ DINER
D56. This and more too. ~ THESE
D60. What she got at the bargain counter. ~ GERM
D62. There is one of these in every talking picture. ~ PLOT
D65. An expression that sounds as if some one was "regusted". ~ PAH
D66. You are working on a small margin now. ~ RIM
D67. An old family connection. ~ APE


